<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuwana</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.) · capital Tuwana</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Tuwana, a Neo-Hittite kingdom around Tyana in southern Cappadocia, whose king Warpalawa is carved on the Ivriz rock relief.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tuwana lay in the plain south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigde">Niğde</a>, in the region the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> called <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tabal">Tabal</a>. It emerged as a separate kingdom in the 8th century BCE around the old city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyana">Tuwanuwa</a>, later Greek Tyana.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warpalawa">Warpalawa</a> ruled with Luwian hieroglyphic inscriptions and is shown on the rock relief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivriz-relief">Ivriz</a> before the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarhunt">Tarhunza</a>, who holds grapes and ears of wheat, symbols of the land's fertility. He paid tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> in 738 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warpalawa">Warpalawa</a>, Tuwana fades from the record during the rise of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-phrygia">Phrygia</a> and the Cimmerian invasions of the 7th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyana">Tyana</a> remained an important city in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Cappadocia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tuwana.jpg" alt="Tuwana" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tyana,_Cappadocia,_Turkey_(23777549058).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tuwana</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Warpalawa, Muwaharani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tyana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-750&amp;focus=tuwana" aria-label="Tuwana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5543 817 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tuwana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5863 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5863 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-750&amp;focus=tuwana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

